        "Enrico Costa",
        "Filippo Frontera",
        "Shrinivas R. Kulkarni"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Afterglow localization converted gamma-ray bursts from mysterious flashes into cosmological astrophysical phenomena.",
        "attribution": "Costa and Frontera were central to the enabling satellite observations, while Kulkarni made decisive optical follow-up contributions.",
        "maturity": "The cosmological origin and afterglow phenomenology are firmly established through many subsequent observations.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The distributed chain of satellite, alert, and follow-up work makes a three-person attribution difficult."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B011",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Christian J. Bordé",
        "David E. Pritchard",
        "Mark A. Kasevich"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry created an exceptionally sensitive measurement method with fundamental and practical reach.",
        "attribution": "Bordé, Pritchard, and Kasevich each made foundational conceptual or experimental contributions to the modern field.",
        "maturity": "The methods are widely implemented in precision gravimetry, inertial sensing, and tests of fundamental physics.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has many essential pioneers, so attribution requires a careful historical review."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B015",
